1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
Hi, if you're like me, you spent hours scrolling on TikTok or Pinterest looking at Minecraft building ideas without the intent to ever actually build them.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:12,000
Now, I don't know about you, but sometimes inspiration just randomly hits.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:15,000
And I have to play Minecraft. Like, that's all that's on my mind.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,000
And lately I've been seeing a whole lot of cottagecore Minecraft content circulating around the internet, getting a lot of views.

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:28,000
And so I downloaded some shaders and launched Minecraft at like 6fps and built this for nostalgia.

6
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:33,100
And if you're like literally any person who's played Minecraft over the years, these houses

7
00:00:33,100 --> 00:00:34,940
were the bomb.

8
00:00:34,940 --> 00:00:37,080
They were like move-in ready.

9
00:00:37,080 --> 00:00:40,900
Of course, there was only room for like three things to fit in there.

10
00:00:40,900 --> 00:00:43,060
And then there was this problem.

11
00:00:43,060 --> 00:00:46,780
It's Minecraft cottagecore aesthetic because the mushroom houses with this aesthetic are

12
00:00:46,780 --> 00:00:48,200
like next level.

13
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:49,200
They are chef's kiss.

14
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:50,780
They are immaculate.

15
00:00:50,780 --> 00:00:55,840
And suddenly your wikiHow tutorial on how to make simple mushroom houses featuring acacia

16
00:00:55,840 --> 00:01:01,060
Plank flooring are more anesthetic than the 2013 mustache trend.

17
00:01:01,060 --> 00:01:09,020
And if this strangely specific scenario applies to you, then boy will you like this tutorial.

18
00:01:09,020 --> 00:01:15,100
So now I present 3 ways to make mushroom houses in Minecraft.

19
00:01:15,100 --> 00:01:17,240
Yaaaaay.

20
00:01:17,240 --> 00:01:19,880
So the first house is pretty easy.

21
00:01:19,880 --> 00:01:24,160
You're gonna want to start out with a statistically impossible mushroom in the middle of a field

22
00:01:24,160 --> 00:01:28,920
and use bone meal to make it big. Now stare at that mushroom until you decide to mutilate

23
00:01:28,920 --> 00:01:34,140
it to look aesthetic, like this. I wanted the mushroom to look a bit different than the

24
00:01:34,140 --> 00:01:39,660
other simple mushroom houses you typically see, so I went for more of a bell-shaped curve

25
00:01:39,660 --> 00:01:45,520
for the top of the mushroom. You could also use one of those mini water bottles for reference.

26
00:01:45,520 --> 00:01:51,160
I modeled mine based on an upside-down flower pot, and I think it turned out pretty good.

27
00:01:51,160 --> 00:01:56,880
Once you have achieved maximum upside down flower pot conformity, then you can start modifying

28
00:01:56,880 --> 00:01:59,340
the stem to make it look stalkier.

29
00:01:59,340 --> 00:02:04,620
I used the mushroom stem block and smooth court stairs, and if you're in survival you'll

30
00:02:04,620 --> 00:02:08,420
probably want to mine a few of the big mushrooms with a silk touch axe to get the materials

31
00:02:08,420 --> 00:02:09,420
for this.

32
00:02:09,420 --> 00:02:13,180
I used birch plank flooring to match the rest of the interior.

33
00:02:13,180 --> 00:02:25,727
Now I really like this house but there not mushroom Sorry for my spore sense of humor Here the big grand reveal of this house Very simple I give it a 8 out of 10

34
00:02:25,727 --> 00:02:26,727
Next house.

35
00:02:26,727 --> 00:02:30,367
This was a little bit more difficult than the last one, and so you're gonna start out by

36
00:02:30,367 --> 00:02:34,887
making a frame that looks like the outline of a handmade cube of playdoh, the kind that

37
00:02:34,887 --> 00:02:38,967
the edges never seem sharp enough, and then start sculpting it to look like the body of

38
00:02:38,967 --> 00:02:43,527
this absurdly priced plastic Minnie Mouse teapot I found on Amazon.

39
00:02:43,527 --> 00:02:49,587
I use smooth quartz blocks, slabs, and stairs to accomplish this, and if you want to use mushroom

40
00:02:49,587 --> 00:02:53,627
stem blocks, I'm sure you could, but I just like quartz because it makes the mushroom

41
00:02:53,627 --> 00:02:57,647
stem seem smooth and continuous, sort of like a differentiable equation.

42
00:02:57,647 --> 00:03:01,007
Also because it's not a pain to get all those stems.

43
00:03:01,007 --> 00:03:04,967
After this, start making the stem taller to look almost like a BB-8, and then give the

44
00:03:04,967 --> 00:03:07,627
wannabe BB-8 some helicopter blades.

45
00:03:07,627 --> 00:03:10,907
These will be the radius of your circular mushroom cap.

46
00:03:10,907 --> 00:03:14,667
Then connect those little helicopter blades to form a circle.

47
00:03:14,667 --> 00:03:20,027
Also I never really found these graphs useful, I always find them on like TikTok, but then

48
00:03:20,027 --> 00:03:23,507
again I always just wing it and it doesn't look very circular so maybe they're helpful

49
00:03:23,507 --> 00:03:26,307
to you, but mine turned out pretty great in the end.

50
00:03:26,307 --> 00:03:31,947
Anyways, start outlining the circle in red mushroom blocks and then be indecisive and then decide

51
00:03:31,947 --> 00:03:37,607
on making the easy way out and make a circular spherical looking mushroom cap.

52
00:03:37,607 --> 00:03:42,607
Mine is about 6 blocks tall, and unlike me, you probably want to make sure there's no weird blocks left exposed.

53
00:03:42,607 --> 00:03:48,607
Then, I made the door and the window and outlined them in the most superior wood type, spruce wood.

54
00:03:48,607 --> 00:03:52,607
I used planks, fences, and stairs as well as oak leaves for the window boxes.

55
00:03:52,607 --> 00:03:57,607
To add some extra whimsy, you can make a dilapidated cobblestone chimney and drape that with oak leaves.

56
00:03:57,607 --> 00:04:04,607
To add some stuff to the interior, I connected the chimney to a fireplace furnace type thing

57
00:04:04,607 --> 00:04:09,067
I added an unnecessary amount of bookshelves and leaves, because when in doubt, add oak leaves

58
00:04:09,067 --> 00:04:09,787
and bookshelves.

59
00:04:09,927 --> 00:04:10,787
That's my motto.

60
00:04:11,487 --> 00:04:15,787
Also, I used flowerpots, turtle eggs, and sea pickles for the inside, and then I discovered

61
00:04:15,787 --> 00:04:20,507
that in 1.16.5, you can place turtle eggs on flowerpots, which is kind of weird.

62
00:04:21,227 --> 00:04:25,507
Anywho, I used vines as a ladder to the attic area, which will be a cute little bedroom.

63
00:04:25,847 --> 00:04:29,767
I outlined the roof with spruce wood and then placed some bookshelves behind them.

64
00:04:30,067 --> 00:04:43,513
This would also be a cool enchanting room but the house demands a bedroom So I added in a skylight which looks good with the shaders and I sure it would look good without them So this is the finished build It only took me about 45 minutes to do in creative so not too bad

65
00:04:43,513 --> 00:04:46,353
Ta-da!

66
00:04:46,353 --> 00:04:50,013
Alright, on to the last one.

67
00:04:50,013 --> 00:04:54,553
This one is kinda hard but not like prior gaming at the end of his TikToks kinda hard.

68
00:04:54,553 --> 00:04:58,553
You're gonna wanna start out with a circular base like such and then add a small circle

69
00:04:58,553 --> 00:05:00,033
attached to it.

70
00:05:00,033 --> 00:05:03,573
Once you have your two little circle buddies, start making them taller and rounder.

71
00:05:03,573 --> 00:05:07,353
I focused on the bigger circle first because it's going to be the centerpiece for the build.

72
00:05:07,353 --> 00:05:11,313
Also, if this video gets 100 likes, I'll make a video of my little sister following this

73
00:05:11,313 --> 00:05:14,873
tutorial to see how she does because she's bad at building and I'm bad at explaining

74
00:05:14,873 --> 00:05:15,873
things.

75
00:05:15,873 --> 00:05:17,153
Okay, back to the tutorial.

76
00:05:17,153 --> 00:05:20,253
If you don't know what shape to make the base of the big mushroom, I referred to the gourd

77
00:05:20,253 --> 00:05:25,993
from the 2007 Disney classic, Secret of the Magic Gourd, and mine turned out pretty alright.

78
00:05:25,993 --> 00:05:29,613
Also I need to know, was anyone else low-key traumatized by that movie?

79
00:05:29,613 --> 00:05:33,313
Because when I first saw that movie, I guess I just thought gourds were strange pumpkins.

80
00:05:33,313 --> 00:05:38,453
So for the longest time, I was fundamentally confused on what gourds were.

81
00:05:38,453 --> 00:05:41,893
So anyway, you're gonna start making another one of those helicopter circle things.

82
00:05:41,893 --> 00:05:45,733
And here's a better explanation using calculus if you want to know how to make circles in

83
00:05:45,733 --> 00:05:46,733
Minecraft.

84
00:05:46,733 --> 00:05:47,733
Presenting.

85
00:05:47,733 --> 00:05:48,733
Building circles.

86
00:05:48,733 --> 00:05:49,733
Yay!

87
00:05:49,733 --> 00:05:52,333
So you're gonna want to start out with this sort of graph paper.

88
00:05:52,333 --> 00:05:54,173
Just trace something circular onto it.

89
00:05:54,173 --> 00:05:57,573
And then fill in the blocks that the circle crosses.

90
00:05:57,573 --> 00:05:59,253
Make sure it all has the same radius.

91
00:05:59,253 --> 00:06:00,633
And then this is where the calculus comes in.

92
00:06:00,633 --> 00:06:04,013
If you look at tangent lines, you know what tangent lines are, I hope.

93
00:06:04,713 --> 00:06:05,673
You probably don't.

94
00:06:06,633 --> 00:06:16,733
But the slope of the tangent line is sort of the slope of, if you were to draw the slope through the grid squares of the circle, this made a lot more sense inside my head.

95
00:06:17,033 --> 00:06:22,553
But you can trace pretty much any circle on the graph paper and repeat this process.

96
00:06:22,653 --> 00:06:26,073
Just make sure the radius is the same and that each quadrant is the same.

97
00:06:26,153 --> 00:06:26,753
And there you go.

98
00:06:26,793 --> 00:06:28,153
You have a circle in Minecraft.

99
00:06:29,253 --> 00:07:19,820
Okay now moving on to our other little mushroom buddy You gonna want to make him a wonky little mushroom that sort of stems off from the big one It really hard to explain how I did this but I would recommend using organic shapes and have the top of the mushroom thinner than the base Then add a tiny little cap For the top of the big mushroom I basically did the same thing as I did for the medium tutorial and despite it being vastly bigger the cap is about the same height Maybe even five blocks this time I kind of wanted to make it sort of sushi like the first build but I lost all my brain cells thinking about calculus If you want the mushroom to be super cottagecore you can add some birch leaves that hang like vines and some hanging lanterns You could also use shroom lights I also added a dilapidated chimney to mash the vibe And then for the interior I started again by connecting the fireplace to the chimney and built a loft area with a ladder leading up to it

100
00:07:19,820 --> 00:07:24,220
And then I got distracted by a wandering trader and quickly resumed my work.

101
00:07:24,640 --> 00:07:27,520
And I used an absurd amount of birch leaves to decorate.

102
00:07:28,420 --> 00:07:31,540
If you can't tell from my Pinterest page, birch leaves are my favorite.

103
00:07:32,060 --> 00:07:36,320
Also, if you're wondering how they move in the wind, that's the shaders, and I use BSL shaders.

104
00:07:36,640 --> 00:07:38,780
As far as I know, they only work for Java Edition.

105
00:07:39,320 --> 00:07:46,180
I made a little kitchen space in front of one of the windows, complete with some cake and some turtle eggs, and then added some seating by the fire.

106
00:07:46,180 --> 00:07:51,540
In front of the other window, I made what HGTV calls a breakfast nook, complete with some plants.

107
00:07:52,020 --> 00:07:56,900
While the bedroom space is smaller than the previous build, I think it turned out nicely and I wouldn't mind living there.

108
00:07:57,440 --> 00:08:06,960
After decorating the bedroom, I remembered there was a bunch of empty space in the loft, which I promptly decorated with barrels because that can be practical when they're not converting all the villagers to fishers.

109
00:08:07,260 --> 00:08:11,260
And I did notice I accidentally made a sort of shrine to Pinocchio, which is cool, I guess.

110
00:08:11,260 --> 00:08:20,020
On the outside, I was going for a house-ish entrance, but I ended up making it more like a hobbit hole with spruce trapdoors and slabs and stairs.

111
00:08:20,420 --> 00:08:27,520
I also added some dark oak for better contrast and ended up expanding the windows downward because it just looked better.

112
00:08:28,280 --> 00:08:32,860
I added some shutters to the bedroom window and shroom lights to better illuminate it at night.

113
00:08:33,440 --> 00:08:36,1000
There was something that was still missing though, and I couldn't decide what to do,

114
00:08:36,1000 --> 00:08:45,360
So I tried this thing I saw on TikTok where you make mushrooms out of end rods and mushroom blocks, which I was a bit skeptical of, but I really liked how they turned out.

115
00:08:46,200 --> 00:08:50,860
So yeah, those are three mushroom houses you could easily build in Minecraft.

116
00:08:51,660 --> 00:08:53,280
Comment below which one you'd live in.

117
00:08:53,400 --> 00:08:57,600
I know I definitely would pick the second one because of the skylight, but that's just me.

118
00:08:57,900 --> 00:09:04,940
